<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Ko Chang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-thai-war">Franco-Thai War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Ko Chang was a naval engagement fought on 17 January 1941 off the island of Ko Chang in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-thailand">Gulf of Thailand</a>, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-thai-war">Franco-Thai War</a>. A French squadron led by the light cruiser Lamotte-Picquet surprised the Thai fleet at anchor and sank or disabled several ships, including the coastal defence ship Thonburi, without losing a vessel. It was the main French success of the war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-ko-chang.jpg" alt="Battle of Ko Chang" width="320" height="271" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Xiengyod (original work by historicair) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Koh_Chang_17_january_1941_(English_version).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-thai-war">Franco-Thai War</a> was going badly for the French on land, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-decoux">Admiral Jean Decoux</a>, Governor General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> and commander of its naval forces, decided to strike at the Thai fleet and coastal cities to clear the way for bombardments in support of a counter-offensive along the Cambodian frontier. On 9 December 1940 he formed a small squadron, the Groupe Occasionnel, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cam-ranh-bay">Cam Ranh Bay</a> under Capitaine de Vaisseau Régis Bérenger: the light cruiser Lamotte-Picquet, built in 1923, the modern avisos Dumont d'Urville and Amiral Charner, and the older avisos Tahure and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a>.</p><p>The Royal Thai Navy had recently acquired ships from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Its core was two Japanese-built armoured coast defence ships, HTMS Thonburi and Sri Ayudhya, each with four 8-inch guns, and the Royal Thai Air Force had over 140 aircraft. Decoux ordered attacks on the coast from Rayong to the Cambodian frontier. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> 130 seaplanes from Ream reported one coastal defence ship and one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torpedo">torpedo</a> boat at Ko Chang, Bérenger chose a dawn attack there, the weaker of the two Thai anchorages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The French closed on the anchorage at 05:30 on 17 January and split into three groups to cover the channels between the islands. A last air reconnaissance found two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torpedo">torpedo</a> boats instead of one, HTMS Chonburi having arrived in the night, and the aircraft's attempt to bomb them cost the French their surprise. Lamotte-Picquet soon sank both torpedo boats with gunfire. At 06:38 Thonburi was sighted heading northwest, and a running fight followed among the islets. An early French shot killed her captain, Commander Luang Phrom Viraphan; by 07:15 she was on fire, and she reached shallow water listing heavily. As the squadron headed home from 08:40, Thai aircraft attacked, and one bomb hit Lamotte-Picquet but failed to explode.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Thonburi, with about 20 dead, was taken in tow by the transport HTMS Chang and run aground on purpose at Laem Ngop, where she capsized; Chonburi lost two men and HTMS Songkhla fourteen. Fearing that further French action might turn back the Thai invasion, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> offered to mediate, and the settlement confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>'s annexation of territory it had lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the late 19th century. After World War II Thailand had to return these gains. Thonburi was raised, repaired in Japan and used as a training ship, and her guns and bridge are preserved at the Royal Thai Naval Academy at Samut Prakan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Ko Chang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…forced the governor-general to admit its troops into the north in September 1940 and the south in 1941, and Thailand attacked in the Franco-Thai War of 1940–1941; the French navy won at <b>Ko Chang</b> in January 1941, but Japanese mediation gave Thailand parts of western Cambodia and Laos.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Military</a></li></ul></section>
